%% md
# Why random splits overstate sign recognition accuracy

{{HEADER}}

The 94,477 `asl-signs` sequences come from only 21 signers, and each signer repeats each sign several times. Split them at random and the test set fills with near-copies of training clips by the same person, so the model is partly graded on recognising people. This notebook trains one small model on the same data under two 5-fold cross-validations, one with random folds and one with folds grouped by signer, and compares the accuracy each reports. Then it traces where the gap comes from.

Data: the `asl-signs` competition files only (CC BY 4.0). Needs a GPU (T4), about 35 minutes on Kaggle. Part 3 of a 5-notebook series.

Contents

1. Setup and data
2. Two ways to make five folds
3. The same model, ten times
4. How far apart the two estimates are
5. Signer by signer
6. Where the gap comes from
7. What we found

%% md
## 1. Setup and data

Preprocessing is the same as in notebooks 1 and 2: frames without a hand dropped, left-dominant sequences mirrored, coordinates centred on the shoulders and scaled by the shoulder width, 32 frames resampled.

